  """
  Get a chronologically sorted timeline of key moments for a Jira incident, optimised for
  the Incident Communication Center (ICC) experience.
  
  Returns structured events sourced from the Jira issue changelog (status, priority, severity,
  responders, assignee changes), SLA cycles, issue creation/resolution timestamps, and
  AIOps investigation lifecycle events. Each event carries an `importance` tier so consumers
  can render top-N summaries deterministically.
  
  When `limit` is provided, the server applies a deterministic trim:
    1. Anchor pass — always include INCIDENT_CREATED and RESOLVED if present.
    2. Importance pass — fill remaining slots by importance DESC, then timestamp DESC.
    3. Quota pass — cap any single category at 2 entries to prevent one chatty
       category from monopolising the view.
    4. Re-sort the selected entries by timestamp ASC for chronological display.
  
  Same-category churn is deduplicated before trimming using a first-or-last occurrence rule:
  state-establishment categories (RESPONDERS_ADDED, ASSIGNEE_ADDED, MAJOR_INCIDENT_DECLARED,
  COMMS_POSTED) keep the first occurrence; current-state categories (PRIORITY_CHANGED, SEVERITY_CHANGED,
  AFFECTED_SERVICES_ADDED) keep the latest — the cumulative latest value already contains
  all prior additions, so older entries are redundant. STATUS_CHANGED is preserved as-is
  (the journey matters) and is bounded by the quota cap.
  
  ### OAuth Scopes
  
  One of the following scopes will need to be present on OAuth requests to get data from this field
  
  * __read:servicedesk-request__
  * __read:request:jira-service-management__
  
  ### Field lifecycle
  
  This field is in the 'EXPERIMENTAL' lifecycle stage
  
  To query this field a client will need to add the '@optIn(to: "AIOpsIncidentTimelineIcc")' query directive to the 'aiops_incidentTimelineIcc' field, or to any of its parents.
  
  The field is extremely unstable. It can go through changes at any moment, and its execution can be slow and/or unreliable. Clients should use it with care and at their own risk!
  """
  aiops_incidentTimelineIcc(
    cloudId: ID! @CloudID(collabContextProduct : JIRA_SERVICE_DESK, owner : "jira"),
    issueKey: String!,
    "Max entries to return after dedup + selection. Null = return all (no trim)."
    limit: Int
  ): AIOpsIncidentTimelineIccResponse @lifecycle(allowThirdParties : false, name : "AIOpsIncidentTimelineIcc", stage : EXPERIMENTAL) @scopes(product : JIRA_SERVICEDESK, required : [READ_SERVICEDESK_REQUEST, READ_REQUEST])
